# test-case-matrix (Cursor)

Produces the scenario list that `react-testing`/`e2e-testing`/
`webapp-testing` implement against — a planning artifact, not automation.
Table column headers stay in English (matches tester convention); cell
content is written in Bahasa Indonesia; translate an English requirement
source when writing it into the matrix.

## Step 1 — Find the requirement source

Priority order: a PRD/ISSUES pair (`docs/prd/**/PRD.md`,
`docs/prd/**/ISSUES.md`) or a BRD read via `brd-reader`, if the
slug/feature matches; a GitHub issue if referenced; any other spec doc that
plausibly covers the feature. If nothing structured exists, ask the user
for a short description rather than inventing requirements — note in the
output that requirements are informal.

## Step 2 — Parameter matrix (when relevant)

Skip for simple single-input features. For features with multiple
interacting variables (order type × payment method, form field ×
validation mode), list each variable and its values, then build a
pairwise-coverage combination table rather than a full cartesian product
past ~3 variables. Reference each combination id in the case's `Test
Variable` column later.

## Step 3 — Extract scenarios

Cover functional (every documented behavior/acceptance criterion), edge
case (boundaries, empty/null, max-size), error handling (invalid input,
failed dependencies, permission denied, concurrency conflicts), and state
transition (any multi-step flow) — don't stop at the happy path. Group into
PB items (one per distinct feature/BRD/PRD), then into `Group No` clusters
sharing the same precondition. Identify source file(s) per scenario via a
quick Glob/Grep pass.

## Step 4 — Write the matrix

One file: `docs/qa/<slug>/test-matrix.md`. Structure: header (feature,
requirement source link, tester/programmer, dates, scope), a two-part
Summary (pass/fail/re-test/skip counts, plus automation-usage percentage —
both recounted live from the table, never maintained separately), then per
PB a mini traceability table (spec → covered? → test case id) followed by
the test case table in this exact column order: `Group No | Feature |
Process No (FC) | TYPE | Test Case ID | Test Variable | Test Case |
Pre-Condition | Test Data | Test Steps | Expected Result | Status |
Evidence | Remarks | Automation Tools | Date | Files | Requirement`.

Key column rules: `Test Case ID` is `TC<GroupNo>-<sequence>` (e.g.
`TC1-1`); `TYPE` is `+`/`-` for positive/negative; `Status` is one of
`⚪ Not Run`/`🟡 Progress`/`✅ Passed`/`❌ Failed`/`🔁 Re-Test`/`⏭ Skip`;
`Automation Tools` is exactly one of `Masuk Test Step`/`Test Data`/
`Tanpa Automation`; `Test Steps`/`Expected Result` are numbered, joined
with `<br>` inside the cell, matched to each other by number. Steps must be
concrete and UI-accurate (real button/field/route names from a quick code
check), not generic ("test the form").

## Step 5 — Report gaps

After writing, tell the user which spec rows have no covering test case
(⚠️ Gap) and which Step 3 categories came up empty for a PB, with your
best read on whether that's genuine inapplicability or under-specified
requirements.

## What this skill does not do

Write test code, execute tests, update Status/Evidence/Date itself, or
decide release-blocking priority.
